-- title:
--   Base change of subspaces over a field commutes with intersection
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a field, let $A$ be a commutative ring carrying the structure of an $R$-algebra, let $M$ be an additive commutative group equipped with an $R$-module structure, and let $p$ and $q$ be $R$-submodules of $M$. For an $R$-submodule $s$ of $M$, write $s_A :=$ `Submodule.baseChange A s` for its base change, the $A$-submodule of $A \otimes_R M$ generated by the elements $1 \otimes x$ with $x \in s$. The theorem asserts the equality of $A$-submodules of $A \otimes_R M$
--   $$(p \sqcap q)_A = p_A \sqcap q_A,$$
--   that is, the base change of the intersection $p \cap q$ coincides with the intersection of the base changes $p_A$ and $q_A$. No finiteness hypothesis is imposed on $M$, $p$ or $q$, and no hypothesis beyond being a commutative $R$-algebra is imposed on $A$; the base ring being a field is what makes the inclusion $p_A \cap q_A \subseteq (p \cap q)_A$, which fails over general base rings, valid.
--
--   This is the standard compatibility of base change of subspaces with intersection over a field, equality resting on the flatness of every module over a field. It is used in the study of modular curves, in [`ModularCurve.eq_zero_of_isInfReductionMap_apply_eq_zero_of_apply_eq_zero_alSlash`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eq_zero_of_isInfReductionMap_apply_eq_zero_of_apply_eq_zero_alSlash), where a vector lying in two base-changed subspaces must be recognised as lying in the base change of their intersection.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Submodule_baseChange_inf.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

set_option autoImplicit false

open scoped TensorProduct

theorem Submodule.baseChange_inf
    {R : Type*} [Field R] (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]
    {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (p q : Submodule R M) :
    (p ⊓ q).baseChange A = p.baseChange A ⊓ q.baseChange A := by sorry
